# Sentinel chain-of-custody demo, environment.
#
# This file lists ONLY what sentinel/ adds. Everything else it needs is already in the
# repo root's .env.example, under the same names, so a filled-in ../.env that already
# drives the Fleet Ops driver needs just the three SENTINEL_TASKING_* values appended.
#
# Nothing real belongs in this file. If you are about to paste a wlp id, an aus id or a
# secret in here rather than into .env, stop.
#
#   set -a; . ../.env; . ./.env; set +a
#
# --- Reused from ../.env.example, listed here only so the mapping is explicit ----------
#
#   OKTA_DOMAIN                  the tenant, hostname only
#   OKTA_SERVICE_CLIENT_ID       the Sentinel Watch Service (an API Services client)
#   OKTA_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET     "
#   OKTA_SERVICE_CLIENT_SCOPE      "   optional, defaults to agent.invoke
#   OKTA_AGENT_OWN_AS_ID         the authorization server protecting the INTAKE AGENT
#                                as a resource, which is where hop 1 is performed
#   OKTA_AGENT_ID                the Sentinel Intake Agent's workload principal id
#   OKTA_AGENT_RESOURCE_URL      the Intake Agent's resource URL, e.g. api://sentinel-intake
#   OKTA_AGENT_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE  the Intake Agent's private key, as a JWK on disk
#
# The Intake Agent is both the audience of hop 1 and the actor of hop 2, which is why it
# reuses the driver's OKTA_AGENT_* names rather than getting its own.

# --- New: the Sentinel Tasking Agent, the privileged target of hop 2 -------------------
#
# The Fleet Ops driver's target is a resource server behind a read or command lane, so
# there is no existing variable that means "the other agent". These three are it.
#
# All three are required. There are deliberately no defaults: a silent default here would
# produce a refusal whose cause is the default rather than the tenant, which is the exact
# opposite of what this app is for.

# The custom authorization server protecting the Tasking Agent.
SENTINEL_TASKING_AS_ID=aus000000000000000000

# The Tasking Agent's resource URL. Sent as `resource` on the exchange, and what Okta
# stamps into the final token's aud. Byte-compare it against the resource indicator on
# the Intake Agent's managed connection: a mismatch surfaces as invalid_target, which
# reads like a permission problem and is not one.
SENTINEL_TASKING_RESOURCE_URL=api://sentinel-tasking

# Space separated. Okta does not down-scope a token-exchange request, so one scope the
# Intake Agent's managed CONNECTION cannot grant fails the whole request rather than
# yielding the grantable subset. Publishing a scope on the authorization server and its
# policy is not sufficient; it has to be on the connection.
SENTINEL_TASKING_SCOPES=sentinel.tasking.dispatch

# --- Optional -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Display names only. Wrong values here are cosmetic.
# SENTINEL_WATCH_NAME=Sentinel Watch Service
# SENTINEL_INTAKE_NAME=Sentinel Intake Agent
# SENTINEL_TASKING_NAME=Sentinel Tasking Agent

# Blanks the tenant host and the wlp/aus/0oa ids out of the endpoint strings the API
# reports, for demos on a shared screen. Claims are never masked: showing exactly what
# the token carries is the point.
# SENTINEL_MASK_IDS=false

# Where the static frontend is served from, echoed as Access-Control-Allow-Origin.
# SENTINEL_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=*

# 8090 rather than 8080, which Bifrost already owns in this repo's compose file.
# PORT=8090
